    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 325\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 8693, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT authorizing and directing the commissioner of environmental\n      conservation to conduct a study on food waste initiatives  and  to\n      provide recommendations based on the study's findings\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would require the Commissioner of the Department of Environ-\nmental  Conservation  (DEC) to conduct a study of food waste initiatives\nand to provide a report to the Governor and the Legislature  along  with\nrecommendations  for reducing food waste. The purpose of the study would\nbe to measure the effectiveness of various  food  waste  initiatives  in\norder to develop statewide policy.\n\n  The  State, through DEC, is already engaged in administering and fund-\ning programs for reducing the amount of food  waste  landfilled  in  the\nState.    Through  the  Municipal  Waste  Reduction  and Recycling State\nAssistance Program, DEC is already  aware  of  initiatives  proposed  by\nmunicipalities  to  promote food donation and organics recycling. In its\nregulatory role, DEC is also actively involved in the local solid  waste\nmanagement  planning  process  and advises solid waste planning units on\ntheir goals for reducing the amount of landfilled waste. DEC also  part-\nners  with  the New York State Pollution Prevention Institute, who main-\ntains a map of organics generators and organic recycling facilities  and\nmaintains a clearinghouse of existing initiatives throughout the State.\n \n  Given  the  amount of existing information on food waste diversion and\nrecycling available on a  State  and  national  level,  the  bill  would\nrequire the State to expend significant resources to study an issue that\nis  well  known  to  the  State  and  would  redirect  staff to identify\nsolutions to a solid waste issue they are already  actively  addressing.\nThis  would  not  be a prudent use of unbudgeted state resources, and on\nthat basis, I am constrained to veto this bill. In addition, since  this\nbill  would  have a fiscal impact on State resources it therefore should\nbe addressed during development of the  state  budget.  Nevertheless,  I\nremain  committed  to working with the Legislature to identify proactive\nsolutions to solid waste management issues in the State and look forward\nto implementing State programs to encourage New Yorkers  to  reduce  the\namount of food waste landfilled each year.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
